concept self_parent {
  name = "self & parent"
  summary = "Keywords that navigate the enclosing scope — self is the current block, parent the one around it."
  related = [{ id: "references", why: "References supports self & parent: &T fields that accept any value structurally satisfying an interface." }]
  audience = :both
  body {
    p <<DOC
      Inside a block, the `self` and `parent` keywords are navigators into the document — the same
      kind of lazy reference a `&T` field holds. `self` resolves to the **current** block (the
      innermost enclosing scope); `parent` resolves to the block **around** it. Both walk the
      lexical scope chain upward, so a field can read a sibling or an ancestor without naming the
      whole path.
    DOC
    h2 "self"
    p <<DOC
      `self` names the current block. Member access off it reads a field declared in the same block
      — handy when a name would otherwise be shadowed, or to be explicit about where a value comes
      from.
    DOC
    code "wcl" {
      source = <<DOC
        box "panel" {
          width  = 480.0
          height = 270.0
          // self.width reads this block's own `width` field.
          ratio  = self.width / self.height
        }
      DOC
    }
    h2 "parent"
    p <<DOC
      `parent` names the enclosing block one level out. Use it to read a value declared on the
      container from within a nested block. Referencing `parent` at the document root is an error —
      there is no scope above it.
    DOC
    code "wcl" {
      source = <<DOC
        service "web" {
          region = "us-east-1"
          metadata {
            // parent.region reaches the surrounding service block.
            inherited_region = parent.region
          }
        }
      DOC
    }
    callout "Navigators, not copies" {
      class = ["note"]
      body = "self and parent reify to references resolved lazily through lexical scope (innermost block out, then the document root) — the same resolution a `&T` field uses. See [References](concept_references)."
    }
  }
}
